findings.body:
Before this PR, the post title was a link to `#/editor/post/{id}`, so moderators could jump to the post. Now the title is a button that calls `onAddFilter('post', ...)`. The editor link is gone, and the only remaining way to reach the post is the "View post" item in the overflow menu, which opens the public URL. This is probably intentional, since the list is now filterable from the row, but it is a visible behaviour change that the PR description does not mention.

::diff{file="apps/posts/src/views/comments/components/comments-list.tsx" lines="236-243"}

If the editor link should stay, add it back as a separate action (for example in the overflow menu). Otherwise, call the behaviour change out in the PR description.

The `<img>` is always rendered with `src` set and is only hidden with `hidden … lg:block`. Browsers still fetch `display:none` images, so every row on mobile downloads a post feature image that is never shown. The list can be long and virtualized, so this adds up in the mobile admin view.

::diff{file="apps/posts/src/views/comments/components/comments-list.tsx" lines="350-358"}

Render the image only at `lg` and up. Either read the breakpoint with a media query hook, or set `loading="lazy"` so the browser defers the request. Note that `loading="lazy"` does not skip `display:none` images on all browsers, so a `matchMedia` check is the more reliable option.

The overflow trigger renders only `<LucideIcon.Ellipsis />` with no text or `aria-label`, so screen readers announce it as an unnamed button. The count indicators (reply, like, and report) are tooltips on plain `div`s, which keyboard users cannot focus, so the tooltip text is not reachable without a mouse.

::diff{file="apps/posts/src/views/comments/components/comments-list.tsx" lines="320-326"}

Add `aria-label="Comment actions"` to the trigger button. For the counts, either make them focusable (for example `tabIndex={0}`) or include the text in a visually hidden span.

Several `className` template strings use `${cond && 'class'}`. When `cond` is false, the string gets the literal text `false` in its class list. This is harmless for styling, but it is a sloppy pattern. The original code already had the same issue, and the PR copies it into new spots. It also sits next to a `ternary` in the reports indicator (line 307), so the style is inconsistent within this file.

::code{file="apps/posts/src/views/comments/components/comments-list.tsx" lines="194-200"}

Use a ternary (`cond ? 'class' : ''`) or `cn()` from `@tryghost/shade`, which the rest of the app already uses.

The old reports line used `text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-500`. The new indicator uses `text-yellow-600` with no dark variant, so the highlight may be low contrast on dark backgrounds. The old code also only showed reports when the count was above zero. The new code always shows the count and highlights it whenever it is truthy, which is a reasonable choice but should be deliberate.

::diff{file="apps/posts/src/views/comments/components/comments-list.tsx" lines="307-307"}

Use the same colour classes as the old code (`text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-500`), or the project's standard warning token.

`formatDate` formats the date with `Intl.DateTimeFormat` and then uses a regex to delete the comma between the day and year. This depends on the exact English output of the current ICU version, and it does not cover other formats. It also leaves the comma before the time in place, so the output is inconsistent. Intl options can produce the wanted format directly. The comment explains the intent, but the logic is fragile.

::diff{file="apps/posts/src/views/comments/components/comments-list.tsx" lines="60-71"}

If the goal is "Dec 17 2025 3:45 PM", build the string with `formatToParts` and join the parts you want, or accept the comma and drop the regex. Note that `formatTimestamp` from `@tryghost/shade` is already imported in this file for the relative time, so pick one date style for this list.

The only test changes are snapshot updates in the backend e2e suite. Nothing in `apps/posts` covers `CommentsList`. The delete regression above would have been caught by a simple test that opens the row menu and confirms the delete dialog. The `formatDate` regex and the clamp detection are also untested. The `comments-ui` e2e moderation test is a different app and does not exercise this view.

::callout{type="info"}
A small React Testing Library test for `CommentsList` that checks the menu items (View post, View member, Delete comment for non-deleted rows) and the Hide/Show buttons would protect this view from similar regressions.
